<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Ialysos</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 408 BCE (approx.) · capital Ialysos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the three ancient cities of Rhodes, on the hill of Philerimos, home of the famous Olympic boxing family of Diagoras.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ialysos lay on the north-west coast of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-philerimos">Mount Philerimos</a>. A Minoan and then Mycenaean settlement flourished there, with rich chamber tombs. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorian Greeks</a> settled, and Ialysos became one of the three cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lindos">Lindos</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kamiros">Kamiros</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorian-hexapolis">Dorian Hexapolis</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city's acropolis sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus-polieus">Zeus Polieus</a> stood on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-philerimos">Philerimos</a>. Ialysos took part in Rhodian trade and colonisation, and came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> before joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a>. Its leading family, the Diagorids, produced Olympic champions, including the boxer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diagoras-of-rhodes">Diagoras</a>, celebrated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a> in 464 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 408 BCE Ialysos joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lindos">Lindos</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kamiros">Kamiros</a> to found the city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, which absorbed its political life. Ialysos continued as a religious centre and later hosted a Byzantine fortress and a monastery of the Knights of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hospitaller-rhodes">Hospitaller Rhodes</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ialysos.jpg" alt="Ialysos" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Mouras · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rho_gen_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 408 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ialysos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ialysus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-654&amp;focus=ialysos" aria-label="Ialysos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5388 864 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ialysos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5708 1120L5591 1068M5708 1120L5703 1128"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5591 1068h0M5703 1128h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5708 1120h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5708 1120h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-654&amp;focus=ialysos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
